---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import ArticleSchema from "../../components/schema/ArticleSchema.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";

const tableOfContents = [
  { id: "intro", label: "Introduction" },
  { id: "evaluation-criteria", label: "How we evaluated GRC tools" },
  { id: "probo", label: "1. Probo: risk-driven GRC, built for AI agents" },
  { id: "vanta", label: "2. Vanta" },
  { id: "drata", label: "3. Drata" },
  { id: "hyperproof", label: "4. Hyperproof" },
  { id: "onetrust", label: "5. OneTrust" },
  { id: "comparison", label: "GRC tools comparison table" },
  { id: "trends", label: "What GRC teams should expect" },
  { id: "conclusion", label: "Final thoughts" },
];
---

<Layout
  title="Top 5 GRC Tools in 2026 for Modern Compliance"
  description="Discover the best GRC tools in 2026. We review Probo, Vanta, Drata, Hyperproof, and OneTrust based on risk management, governance, and compliance capabilities."
>
  <ArticleSchema
    title="Top 5 GRC Tools in 2026: A Practical Guide for Modern Risk & Compliance Teams"
    description="Discover the best GRC tools in 2026. We review Probo, Vanta, Drata, Hyperproof, and OneTrust based on risk management, governance, and compliance capabilities."
    url="https://www.probo.com/hub/top-grc-tools-2026"
    image="https://www.probo.com/og.png"
    datePublished="2026-01-01"
    dateModified="2026-11-09"
  />
  <main class="container my-10 sm:my-20">
    <Breadcrumbs
      items={[
        { label: "Hub", href: "/hub" },
        { label: "Top 5 GRC tools in 2026" },
      ]}
      class="mb-6"
    />
    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="mx-auto mb-12 max-w-[900px] text-center sm:mb-16">
      <h1
        class="text-foreground mb-6 text-[2.5rem] leading-[1.1] font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] sm:text-[3.5rem]"
      >
        Top 5
        <span class="highlight-text">GRC tools</span>
        in 2026
      </h1>
      <p class="text-muted-foreground text-lg">
        Governance, Risk & Compliance (GRC) has changed dramatically over the
        past few years. What was once a discipline focused on passing audits and
        producing documentation has become a strategic function, deeply
        connected to business decisions, security posture, and operational
        resilience.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Content with TOC -->
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-10 lg:flex-row lg:gap-16">
      <!-- Table of Contents - Sidebar -->
      <aside class="shrink-0 lg:w-56">
        <nav class="lg:sticky lg:top-24">
          <div class="flex flex-col gap-2">
            {
              tableOfContents.map((item) => (
                <button
                  type="button"
                  data-section={item.id}
                  class="toc-link cursor-pointer border-none bg-transparent p-0 text-left text-sm leading-5 text-[#6b716a] transition-colors hover:text-[#141e12]"
                >
                  {item.label}
                </button>
              ))
            }
          </div>
        </nav>
      </aside>

      <!-- Main Content -->
      <article class="max-w-[720px] flex-1">
        <!-- Introduction -->
        <section id="intro" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            The new reality of GRC
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              In 2026, organizations are no longer asking "How do we pass this
              audit?" — they are asking:
            </p>
            <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span>Which risks actually matter to our business?</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span>Who owns them?</span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                <span>
                  How do we make informed decisions under regulatory pressure?</span
                >
              </li>
            </ul>
            <p>
              Choosing the right GRC tool is therefore no longer about
              checklists or automation alone. It is about structure, clarity,
              and accountability.
            </p>
            <p>
              In this article, we review the top 5 GRC tools in 2026, based on
              how well they support real governance, actionable risk management,
              and scalable compliance. We also include a detailed comparison to
              help you understand which platform best fits your organization's
              maturity and regulatory environment.
            </p>
            <p>
              If you're just getting started, you may also want to read about
              <a
                href="/blog/2025-09-11-what-are-the-steps-toward-compliance"
                class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                >the steps toward compliance</a
              >.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong class="text-foreground">Disclosure:</strong> Probo publishes
              this guide and is one of the tools reviewed. We put Probo first and
              say plainly where Vanta, Drata, Hyperproof or OneTrust fit better. If
              you are looking for a SOC 2 or ISO 27001 platform rather than a GRC
              program, read our
              <a
                href="/hub/vanta-alternatives-2026"
                class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                >Vanta alternatives comparison</a
              >
              instead.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <!-- How We Evaluated GRC Tools -->
        <section id="evaluation-criteria" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-6 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            How we evaluated GRC tools in 2026
          </h2>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Before ranking the tools, it is important to clarify the criteria
              used. This article does not focus on feature quantity alone.
              Instead, we evaluated each platform based on five core principles
              that matter in 2026:
            </p>

            <div class="mt-6 space-y-6">
              <div>
                <h3 class="text-foreground mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">
                  1. Risk-centered design
                </h3>
                <p>
                  Does the tool treat risk as a first-class object, or is risk
                  simply inferred from compliance controls?
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <h3 class="text-foreground mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">
                  2. Governance capabilities
                </h3>
                <p>
                  Can organizations define ownership, decision workflows, and
                  accountability — or is governance left outside the tool?
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <h3 class="text-foreground mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">
                  3. Regulatory coverage and adaptability
                </h3>
                <p>
                  How well does the platform support multiple frameworks,
                  especially evolving European regulations such as GDPR, NIS2,
                  DORA, and ISO standards?
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <h3 class="text-foreground mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">
                  4. Usability for real teams
                </h3>
                <p>
                  Can security, compliance, leadership, and operations teams
                  actually use the tool day-to-day?
                </p>
              </div>

              <div>
                <h3 class="text-foreground mb-2 text-lg font-semibold">
                  5. Scalability without complexity
                </h3>
                <p>
                  Does the platform scale with organizational maturity without
                  becoming heavy, rigid, or enterprise-only?
                </p>
              </div>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-6">
              With these criteria in mind, here is our ranking.
            </p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <!-- 1. Probo -->
        <section id="probo" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            1. Probo: risk-driven GRC, built for AI agents
          </h2>
          <div class="mb-6">
            <span class="inline-block text-sm font-medium text-[#5d770d]">
              Best for: Organizations that want structured, risk-driven GRC
              without enterprise complexity, run by their team and their AI
              agents together</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              <a
                href="https://www.probo.com/"
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                class="font-semibold text-[#5d770d] hover:underline">Probo</a
              >
              comes first on our list because it reflects what GRC has become in 2026:
              a continuous, decision-oriented discipline, not a periodic compliance
              exercise.
            </p>
            <p>
              Rather than starting from audits or evidence collection, Probo is
              built around a clear and explicit risk model. Risks are
              identified, owned, evaluated, and linked to controls, policies,
              and regulatory obligations. Governance is not an afterthought — it
              is embedded directly into how GRC operates.
            </p>

            <!-- A Risk-First Foundation -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                A risk-first foundation
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Most GRC tools historically evolved from compliance workflows.
                Risk was introduced later, often as a scoring layer on top of
                controls. Probo takes the opposite approach.
              </p>
              <p class="mb-3">In Probo:</p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Risks are explicitly defined and structured</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Each risk has clear ownership</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Controls exist to mitigate specific risks</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Compliance frameworks map onto this structure — not the
                    other way around</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">
                This makes risk understandable, explainable, and actionable,
                both for operational teams and leadership.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Governance Built Into the Platform -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Governance built into the platform
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Governance is often the missing layer in GRC tools. Many
                platforms store information but leave decision-making outside
                the system. Probo embeds governance directly into GRC workflows:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Clear responsibility assignment</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Review and validation processes</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Structured decision points</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Traceability from risk to decision to action</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">
                This allows organizations to move from documentation to actual
                governance.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Designed for European Regulatory Reality -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Designed for European regulatory reality
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Probo is built with European organizations in mind. This is
                increasingly important in 2026 as EU regulations continue to
                expand in scope and enforcement. Probo supports:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>GDPR</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>ISO 27001 and related standards</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>NIS2</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>DORA</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>And evolving European compliance requirements</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">
                More importantly, these frameworks are not treated as isolated
                checklists. They are mapped into a unified risk and governance
                structure, reducing duplication and long-term maintenance cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Simple, Modern, and Scalable -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Simple, modern, and scalable
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Probo avoids the two extremes that dominate the market:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Overly simple compliance automation tools</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Heavy, enterprise-only GRC platforms</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">Instead, it offers:</p>
              <ul class="mt-3 ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>A modern, intuitive interface</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Enough structure for mature risk management</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Flexibility to scale without re-implementing everything</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">
                This makes Probo suitable for organizations that are growing in
                complexity but still value speed and clarity. Read more about
                <a
                  href="/blog/2025-02-04-the-case-for-open-source-compliance"
                  class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                  >the case for open-source compliance</a
                >
                and see how
                <a
                  href="/stories/vybe-soc2"
                  class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                  >Vybe got SOC 2 certified</a
                >
                using Probo.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Built for AI Agents -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Built for AI agents
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Probo is designed so AI agents can work on your GRC program
                together with your team. Every risk, control, policy, vendor and
                audit is available through:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    >A full <a
                      href="/docs/developers/graphql"
                      class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline">GraphQL API</a
                    ></span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    >An <a
                      href="/docs/developers/api/mcp/overview"
                      class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline">MCP server</a
                    > with 400+ tools for Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor, VS Code and other
                    assistants</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span
                    >The <a
                      href="/docs/developers/cli/overview"
                      class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline">prb CLI</a
                    > for terminals, scripts and CI pipelines</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">
                An agent can assess a new risk, map it to controls, draft the
                policy update and prepare evidence for review, while the risk
                owner keeps the decision. Agents act with the permissions of the
                person who connected them, so governance stays in place.
              </p>
            </div>

            <!-- Honest downside -->
            <div class="mt-8">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                The honest downside
              </h3>
              <p>
                Probo is built for startups and growing companies, with a sweet
                spot under 500 people. A large enterprise running many
                frameworks across global subsidiaries will find OneTrust or
                Hyperproof more mature for that scale today.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <!-- 2. Vanta -->
        <section id="vanta" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            2. Vanta
          </h2>
          <div class="mb-6">
            <span class="inline-block text-sm font-medium text-[#5d770d]">
              Best for: Startups and fast-growing companies focused on audit
              readiness</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Vanta remains one of the most visible names in the GRC ecosystem,
              particularly among startups. Its strength lies in automation —
              especially for SOC 2 and ISO certifications.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-6">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Strengths
              </h3>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Fast initial setup</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Automated evidence collection</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Strong auditor ecosystem</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Widely recognized by auditors and investors</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-6">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Limitations in 2026
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                While Vanta is effective for early-stage compliance, its model
                shows limitations as organizations mature:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Risk management is secondary</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Governance workflows are minimal</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    The platform is optimized for passing audits, not for
                    long-term risk strategy</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">
                Vanta works well when compliance is the primary goal. It becomes
                less suitable when organizations need to structure risk
                ownership and governance at scale. We explore this further in
                <a
                  href="/blog/2025-02-17-why-a-one-size-fit-all-solution-like-vanta-is-not-ideal"
                  class="text-[#5d770d] hover:underline"
                  >why a one-size-fits-all solution is not ideal</a
                >.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <!-- 3. Drata -->
        <section id="drata" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            3. Drata
          </h2>
          <div class="mb-6">
            <span class="inline-block text-sm font-medium text-[#5d770d]">
              Best for: Companies looking for compliance automation with a
              modern UX</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Drata follows a similar philosophy to Vanta, offering continuous
              compliance monitoring and integrations with common SaaS tools.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-6">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Strengths
              </h3>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Clean interface</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Solid automation capabilities</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Competitive alternative to Vanta</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-6">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Limitations in 2026
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">
                Drata, like most compliance-first platforms, struggles with:
              </p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Deep risk modeling</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Governance workflows</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Complex regulatory mapping beyond standard frameworks</span
                  >
                </li>
              </ul>
              <p class="mt-4">
                For teams whose primary need is audit efficiency, Drata is a
                reasonable option. For teams aiming to operationalize GRC beyond
                audits, it remains limited.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <hr class="border-border-low mb-12" />

        <!-- 4. Hyperproof -->
        <section id="hyperproof" class="mb-12 scroll-mt-24">
          <h2
            class="text-foreground mb-2 text-2xl font-semibold tracking-[-0.01em] sm:text-[1.75rem]"
          >
            4. Hyperproof
          </h2>
          <div class="mb-6">
            <span class="inline-block text-sm font-medium text-[#5d770d]">
              Best for: Mid-market organizations wanting more structure than
              compliance tools</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="text-muted-foreground space-y-4 leading-relaxed">
            <p>
              Hyperproof occupies a middle ground between automation tools and
              enterprise GRC platforms.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-6">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Strengths
              </h3>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Broader framework coverage</span>
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Better risk capabilities than pure automation tools</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>Suitable for regulated industries</span>
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <div class="mt-6">
              <h3 class="text-foreground mb-3 text-lg font-semibold">
                Limitations in 2026
              </h3>
              <p class="mb-4">Despite its strengths, Hyperproof still:</p>
              <ul class="ml-0 list-none space-y-3">
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Treats compliance as the core organizing principle</span
                  >
                </li>
                <li class="flex items-start gap-2">
                  <span class="text-muted-foreground">•</span>
                  <span>
                    Adds risk and governance on top, rather than embedding them</span
                  >
                </li>
